"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about number line with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOn 0 to 1 divided into 4 equal intervals, marks are 0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1. 3/4 is three jumps of one quarter from 0.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlace 1/3 and 2/3 on a 0–1 line partitioned into thirds. Which is closer to 1?\n\nFEEDBACK\nMarks at one-third and two-thirds; 2/3 is closer to 1.","explain":"Fractions on a number line sit at equal interval marks between 0 and 1 (or beyond for improper later). Position matches equal partitions of the segment.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than guessing digits by shape alone. Keep every step justified.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the places, values or distances that carry the method. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your working with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your reasoning? If a digit or place is uncertain, identify what would have to be regrouped or counted again. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Placing 1/3 near 3 because the numerator is 1 and denominator 3 ignores equal spacing. Correct the value or method, not just the appearance of the digits.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Fraction marks on a partitioned number line.","caption":"Equal intervals place the fractions.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">0 to 1</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">four equal jumps</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Marks</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">1/4; 2/4; 3/4; 1</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: On 0 to 1 divided into 4 equal intervals, marks are 0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1. 3/4 is three jumps of one quarter from 0. Then write your own working.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which number or place matters most.\nJuno: Name the places, values or distances that carry the method before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: On 0 to 1 divided into 4 equal intervals, marks are 0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1. 3/4 is three jumps of one quarter from 0. Then write your own working."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid.","options":["Make the method visible: name the values, places or steps you used and say why that step is valid","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no working"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about number line with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nOn 0 to 1 divided into 4 equal intervals, marks are 0, 1/4, 2/4, 3/4, 1. 3/4 is three jumps of one quarter from 0. Look at each number, place or distance that carries the method.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite the key numbers from the model and say what each one represents in the method.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful working names the quantities and the reason for each step, not only the final answer.","explain":"Make the 